  "expected_findings": [],
  "reference_answer": "No intervention. Ceftriaxone is a reasonable choice for uncomplicated cystitis even with an AmpC-risk organism; the oral options are resistant, and she cannot take oral medication. The AmpC rule must not fire for cystitis without upper tract or bloodstream involvement.",
  "rationale": "The negative pair of the Enterobacter bacteremia case. IDSA guidance limits its caution about ceftriaxone for moderate to high AmpC-risk organisms to invasive infections; for uncomplicated cystitis ceftriaxone may be reasonable. A rule that fires on organism and drug alone, ignoring the site, fails this case. IV to PO does not apply: she is not taking anything by mouth and no susceptible oral agent exists.",
  "citations": [
    {
